which we'll talk about later.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.640
And we also have App
Engine support for you.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.070
So you can run your
local dev app server

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:58.204
for App Engine standard.

00:10:58.204 --> 00:11:00.620
You can deploy both the App
Engine standard and App Engine

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:01.390
Flex.

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:03.362
For local run for
Flex, obviously it's

00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:04.570
sort of your own environment.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:08.851
So we let you figure
that out for yourself.

00:11:08.851 --> 00:11:10.660
AMIR ROUZROKH:
Actually, can describe,

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:13.890
how do we get this plugin to,
you know, appear on IntelliJ?

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.820
PATRICK FLYNN: Oh yeah, sure.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.400
Most of you will probably
know, as you're IntelliJ users,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:21.550
that JetBrains has a plug-in
repository that you can access

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.670
through preferences, or
settings, in another operating

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:25.480
system.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.480
You just go to
Browse Repositories.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.020
Search for Google Cloud.

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:35.400
And you'll see our
tool right there.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.927
Install, Restart, and
you're good to go.

00:11:37.927 --> 00:11:39.510
So what I'm going
to do first is we're

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:43.130
going to take our
application and deploy it

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:44.000
to App Engine Flex.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.862
So this is our deploy UI.

00:11:49.862 --> 00:11:51.570
The first, probably,
most important thing

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:54.028
that you need to decide when
you're deploying to App Engine

00:11:54.028 --> 00:11:57.800
Flex is, what are you
deploying to App Engine Flex.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.030
There is a peculiarity
about IntelliJ--

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:02.310
this is ultimate
edition by the way--

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:04.770
where if you're
building a web project

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:07.960
it will automatically create
a web artifact for you

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:11.250
that people often
use for deployment.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.500
But it does not do
that for jar based

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.940
projects, which typical
Spring boot projects are.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:20.580
What we've done is we actually
inspect your Maven model

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:23.310
and figure out what
your primary target is

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:24.910
and allow you to deploy that.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:28.470
So what you see here is the
option of basically selecting

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:30.600
that Maven build for
this project, which

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:31.980
is called Spring Boot Simple.

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:34.512
You can also just pick
a file system jar/war

00:12:34.512 --> 00:12:36.720
anywhere in your file system,
and we'll package that,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:37.690
and deploy it for you.

00:12:41.406 --> 00:12:42.780
Next thing you
need to do is pick

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:46.500
which GCP project you want
to deploy your project to.

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:50.810
So it's a real
pain in the butt--

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:51.630
[LAUGHING]

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:54.600
yeah, sorry there--
to actually have

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.750
to configure authentication
for tools typically, right?

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:00.720
You have to either create a
service account or application

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.450
default credentials all
on your cloud provider

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:06.330
and put that in a
key file somewhere.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.750
We take all that pain away
by having this login widget,

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:12.930
which, if I hit Add
Account will take you

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.180
through a web flow
for authentication,

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:16.740
and store the
credentials locally.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.560
So your session is
just there for as long

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.460
as you want it to be.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.390
I'm going to cancel this
because I've done this already.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.974
So we have this user.

00:13:26.974 --> 00:13:28.640
This is my personal
account, by the way.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:32.140
And I'm going to pick the
project I want to deploy to.

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.640
Because this project has already
been enabled for App Engine

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.760
you can see which region
it's deploying to.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.710
And then we see
that the environment

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.680
is App Engine Flexible,
because we also

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:44.000
support Engine standard.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.580
If your app had App
Engine Web XML then

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:48.750
you would see App
Engine standard here.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.500
And finally, we have
the version field,

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.690
which I'm going to
overwrite in this case

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:56.600
to 5.8, which I think is
the version of the code

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:58.825
that I've got checked out.

00:13:58.825 --> 00:13:59.950
AMIR ROUZROKH: So Patrick--

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:00.500
PATRICK FLYNN: Sure.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:01.708
AMIR ROUZROKH: Just to like--

00:14:01.708 --> 00:14:04.250
I mean, so this is, I have
a version already installed.

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:08.840
I can just, basically over here,
version up right from the IDE,

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.770
and deploy the next
versions going forward.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:14.577
So my, let's say, current
production is 5.7.

00:14:14.577 --> 00:14:15.410
Something like that?

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:16.243
PATRICK FLYNN: Sure.

00:14:16.243 --> 00:14:16.911
Absolutely.

00:14:16.911 --> 00:14:19.160
We're going to talk a little
bit more about versioning

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:21.990
and the App Engine model
for services and versions

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:22.490
in a second.

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:23.240
AMIR ROUZROKH: OK.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:27.962
PATRICK FLYNN: So I'm going
to kick off this deployment.

00:14:27.962 --> 00:14:29.420
So what we'll do
is we'll make sure

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:33.380
that your Maven
artifact has been built,

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:36.920
which takes just obviously
a few seconds, hopefully.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:39.660
And then we actually
package source context

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:41.900
into it, which we'll also
talk about when we show you

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:43.160
the Cloud Debugger.

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:45.140
And start the
deployment process.

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.960
AMIR ROUZROKH: Patrick,
so in the background,

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:53.720
you know what [INAUDIBLE]
says building and pushing

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:57.050
image for service service,
or this micro servers,

00:14:57.050 --> 00:15:01.490
and this is a container
based sort of deployment,

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:05.300
but you really didn't
have to deal with Docker,

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.580
Docker Push Image Hub, like
you know, all that stuff.

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:08.070
PATRICK FLYNN: That's right.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:09.290
AMIR ROUZROKH: You just went
from code to containers.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:10.123
How did that happen?

00:15:10.123 --> 00:15:13.370
PATRICK FLYNN: The sort of
vision for App Engine Flexible

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:17.630
is to have standard
infrastructure for running

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:18.830
containers.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:21.650
But what they want to
do is make it as easy

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:24.680
as possible for people to use
a platform without knowing

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:26.310
anything about containers.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:28.100
So if you don't
want to customize

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:30.980
your image with some native
dependency you don't have to.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.950
You don't need to have a Docker
or file, or even app gammel.

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.560
And you don't even need to
have Docker installed locally,

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:40.340
because we have a Cloud build
service, which will orchestrate

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:43.480
into the deployment so that we
can produce the right Docker

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:44.270
artifact for you.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:45.145
AMIR ROUZROKH: I see.

00:15:45.145 --> 00:15:47.660
PATRICK FLYNN: So that's
what's happening right now.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:50.630
We're actually streaming
the results of the Cloud

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:54.752
build for the Docker build
back into the console.

00:15:54.752 --> 00:15:57.210
But it's actually all happening
on the Cloud at the moment.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.940
AMIR ROUZROKH: So you
built a container,

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.064
you're pushed into through
a repository, the Google

00:16:01.064 --> 00:16:04.160
Container Repository, and
you made it to go live,

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:08.110
and you hooked up all the
networking, and all of that.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:08.610
OK.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:09.443
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah.

00:16:09.443 --> 00:16:11.480
So because this is not
App Engine standard

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:14.210
and because it's sort of
built on the basic building

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:17.385
blocks of virtual machines
you see the products

00:16:17.385 --> 00:16:19.760
that you're most familiar with
at the base of the pyramid

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:20.375
we looked at earlier.

00:16:20.375 --> 00:16:21.250
AMIR ROUZROKH: Right.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:24.220
PATRICK FLYNN: The cost
of spinning everything up

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:27.110
is a little larger
than for App Engine

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:30.050
standard which uses shared
resources for deployments.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:31.130
AMIR ROUZROKH: Correct.

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:33.171
PATRICK FLYNN: So it's
updating the service here.

00:16:33.171 --> 00:16:36.740
I'm going to go over
to the Cloud Console

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.020
and show you the
App Engine console.

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:41.930
This is actually
some deployments

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:45.740
that I did earlier
just so that we

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:50.432
didn't have to wait too long for
the new version to be serving.

00:16:50.432 --> 00:16:51.890
What you can see
here is that we've

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:55.130
got version 5.8 serving,
and version 5.7 serving,

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:56.780
version 5.6 serving.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.050
The traffic allocation's
100% on 5.7.

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:02.420
This is basically your
version that all your users

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:03.710
are currently seeing.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.650
We've done a deployment to
5.8 that nobody is currently

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:07.510
seeing.

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:10.990
So App Engine
provides a model that

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:13.750
helps you with a
microservices architecture

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:17.260
through our services so
that you could basically

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:20.270
label your deployments
with a specific service.

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:26.130
And if you go into your
services list over here

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:28.349
you'll see all your
services and versions.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:30.390
AMIR ROUZROKH: So you have
one service right now,

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:31.350
and one version.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:33.597
Now, what I usually
do is I don't

00:17:33.597 --> 00:17:35.430
want to send all the
traffic to the new one,

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:36.721
because things may be breaking.

00:17:36.721 --> 00:17:40.187
Can we do 5% of traffic, maybe?

00:17:40.187 --> 00:17:41.520
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:47.340
When I worked in the olden
days on Java Backend,

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:51.990
trying to set up either
experimentation features, or AB

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.664
testing, or just
release verification

00:17:54.664 --> 00:17:56.580
was so difficult we
didn't even bother, right?

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:57.560
AMIR ROUZROKH: Right.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:00.300
PATRICK FLYNN: We would
just deploy and roll out.

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.020
And if there was a
bug rollback, right?

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:06.660
But now with App Engine Flex
and App Engine Standard,

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.630
it's really easy to just
change the traffic mix

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:13.320
so that a really small
proportion of your users

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.040
start seeing the new code.

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:18.296
So we're going to save that.

00:18:18.296 --> 00:18:22.540
And in a matter of seconds
the App Engine front ends

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.460
will start sending some of your
traffic to your new version.

00:18:28.615 --> 00:18:29.240
Let's see here.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:30.700
Refresh the page.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:31.340
There you go.

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:31.840
All right.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:35.460
So you have 9% of your traffic
served by this new version.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:39.040
AMIR ROUZROKH: And Patrick,
let's say this is broken.

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:40.300
Can we roll back, please?

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.133
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah,
sure I'll roll it back.

00:18:42.133 --> 00:18:43.920
We select that.

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:44.690
Click over here.

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:48.010
Migrate the traffic back.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:52.840
I should also show you, this is
just a silly, hello world app.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:56.810
But this is v5.7.

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:59.470
Now, let's say we've
verified that 5.8 is good,

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:01.480
and we want to send 100%
of the traffic to it.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:02.990
We can do that.

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:03.850
Migrate the traffic.

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:10.867
Should be only a
couple of seconds.

00:19:10.867 --> 00:19:11.700
AMIR ROUZROKH: Yeah.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.840
It shouldn't take more
than just a few moments.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:14.430
There we go.

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:14.660
PATRICK FLYNN: OK.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:17.530
100% of our traffic is now
being served by the new version.

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:21.192
If I go back here I see that
5.8 is the version being

00:19:21.192 --> 00:19:22.150
served to my end users.

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:23.220
AMIR ROUZROKH: Awesome.

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:27.730
All right.

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:31.180
So Patrick, you know,
let's say 5.8 was

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:32.527
a really successful release.

00:19:32.527 --> 00:19:33.360
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:35.568
AMIR ROUZROKH: And it's
getting really, really famous

00:19:35.568 --> 00:19:38.400
and people are just
coming right to our app.

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:39.607
What do I do?

00:19:39.607 --> 00:19:41.190
PATRICK FLYNN: That's
a good question.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:43.140
This is another
thing that when you

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:44.800
were faced with this
issue in the past

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:46.180
it was a real headache.

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:48.490
You don't have enough capacity.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:51.550
Either, your consumer app that's
been more successful than you

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:52.445
had hoped for, right?

00:19:52.445 --> 00:19:53.320
AMIR ROUZROKH: Right.

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:56.290
PATRICK FLYNN: Or you're
getting really spiky traffic

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:59.740
where you need a
lot of resources

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.430
at a certain time of day, but
not at the rest of the day.

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:04.840
What you really wish
you could have is not

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:08.650
worry about capacity and have
capacity managed for you.

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:10.940
And App Engine
actually does that.

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:13.840
So what we'll do
is we'll show you

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.304
that we've got the versions.

00:20:16.304 --> 00:20:18.720
Let's go to the instances and
take a quick look over here.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:22.520
We see that there are currently
two instances running for--

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:23.950
yeah, that's version 5.8.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:25.780
Good.

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:27.400
Refresh.

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:30.260
So by default, without
any configuration,

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.122
we'll run two instances
just for backup.

00:20:32.122 --> 00:20:34.330
If there's an issue you
still couldn't serve traffic.

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:37.272
But what we'll do now is--

00:20:37.272 --> 00:20:38.980
let me just actually
show you real quick.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.160
You saw it earlier.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:44.260
There's an endpoint
on this web app

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:50.140
that is very slow and very
computationally expensive.

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:52.512
So what we're going to do--

00:20:52.512 --> 00:20:54.970
sometimes it comes back really
quick and sometimes it comes

00:20:54.970 --> 00:21:00.080
back really slow, because
it's just calculating digests

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:04.270
of numbers and looking for one
that starts with 007 on a loop.

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:08.584
So it really taxes the CPU.

00:21:08.584 --> 00:21:10.000
So what I'm going
to do now is I'm

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:16.220
going to use Apache Bench to
simulate 10,000 requests, 20

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:18.040
requests at a time.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:21.370
I'm going to go
ahead and start that.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:24.710
AMIR ROUZROKH: So
I guess, Patrick,

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:27.200
what I saw here, if you
didn't do any configurations

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.900
around capacity
provisioning, I didn't

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:33.080
see anywhere you telling us
this could or could not happen,

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.150
or what sort of version this is.

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:38.687
So how does the back end
know that this is going

00:21:38.687 --> 00:21:39.770
to, you know, be an issue?

00:21:39.770 --> 00:21:41.570
PATRICK FLYNN: That's
a good question.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:46.760
I think by default
the CPU load is

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:50.360
what the autoscaler is
going to use as a signal

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:52.610
for spinning up extra instances.

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:54.790
But you have a lot
of control over that.

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:57.140
If you specify your
own app yaml you

00:21:57.140 --> 00:21:59.530
can do it based off of
[INTERPOSING VOICES] PPS.

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:00.600
So all kinds of things.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:01.350
AMIR ROUZROKH: OK.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:02.180
That's awesome.

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:03.500
PATRICK FLYNN: So let's
go back and take a look

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:04.750
at our instances really quick.

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:11.330
So you can see now that App
Engine has already started

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:13.332
spinning up a new instance.

00:22:13.332 --> 00:22:15.290
AMIR ROUZROKH: Sorry,
I'm going to make a note.

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:16.880
It says no requests
