00:22:28.435 --> 00:22:29.226
PATRICK FLYNN: Yep.

00:22:29.226 --> 00:22:32.300
So now this is really
taxing the system.

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:35.520
And to make sure that we
keep on serving traffic

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:39.050
in a reasonable latencies
where the system is continuing

00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:40.940
to spin up instances,
and at some point

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:43.370
is going to find that it's
handling the traffic well

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:44.990
enough, and it's going to stop.

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:45.750
AMIR ROUZROKH:
All load balanced.

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:46.874
Can you do another refresh?

00:22:46.874 --> 00:22:48.590
So I'm thinking the last--

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:51.182
we spun up from 2, 2, 6 to 7.

00:22:51.182 --> 00:22:52.140
And two more coming up.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:53.581
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah, and OK.

00:22:53.581 --> 00:22:54.080
Let's see.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.320
Seven of them are
already serving.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:00.025
So these instances get spun
up, and work a lot faster than,

00:23:00.025 --> 00:23:01.150
for instance, a deployment.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:01.820
It's not like
you're going to have

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:04.245
to wait many minutes before
you're serving the new traffic

00:23:04.245 --> 00:23:05.120
with these instances.

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:07.300
AMIR ROUZROKH: Write that script
and get it back, and set up.

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:09.440
PATRICK FLYNN: It gives
you a lot of agility.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:12.560
And when the traffic
stops App Engine will

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:15.270
scale down so that you're not
spending more than you need to.

00:23:15.270 --> 00:23:16.770
AMIR ROUZROKH: Just
for my pleasure,

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:18.200
