00:23:26.926 --> 00:23:28.081
OK.

00:23:28.081 --> 00:23:28.580
All right.

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:30.770
I think that was it for
this part of the demo.

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:31.936
Should we go back to slides?

00:23:31.936 --> 00:23:32.810
AMIR ROUZROKH: Yeah.

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:33.020
Slides please.

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:33.260
OK.

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:33.760
Cool.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.480
So let me just take
a second to describe

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.410
what we did right now, right?

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:40.550
We started off with
a plain application.

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:42.920
We containerized
it without really

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:45.080
having to interact with Docker.

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:49.310
We employed a microservices
architecture in the background.

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:51.860
We have a repository that
has our container in it

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:53.900
for future reference.

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:56.570
The container went
online and went healthy,

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.220
with two instances.

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:04.700
We did a AB test to see if
the version of the service

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:05.840
is actually what we want.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:07.490
We verified that it was.

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:09.620
Then we migrated
everything over.

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:13.880
Then we loaded the
microservice and it scaled up.

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:15.440
I mean, think
about how much time

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:18.080
it would take for you
to go through this cycle

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:21.050
if you would want to do this
through the traditional way

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:25.910
of handling an end to end
infrastructure for application

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:26.960
development?

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:31.370
This took about nine minutes,
I think, on my clock over here.

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:32.690
So this is pretty good.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:35.920
And it's a lot of addition to
productivity for developers.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:39.140
So developer time is now free
to work on what's critical,

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:40.640
and that's your business.

00:24:40.640 --> 00:24:42.740
Not on the other stuff.

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:45.180
And so if we move back to
what I showed you guys.

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:47.450
And we were up here, right?

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:49.209
Now, the question may be, OK.

00:24:49.209 --> 00:24:50.750
You know this is a
lot of good magic.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:51.740
I really like it.

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:53.720
But I really want more control.

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:56.060
I want to actually have
my containers set up

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:57.890
in different regions.

00:24:57.890 --> 00:24:59.515
I want to have these
containers to have

00:24:59.515 --> 00:25:02.855
a certain level of redundancy.

00:25:02.855 --> 00:25:04.730
You just really want to
have all the control.

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:05.396
All right, cool.

00:25:05.396 --> 00:25:07.580
And sure, you made the
App Engine decision first

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:09.620
when you were getting in.

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:13.340
But now you can just drop
down to the container level

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:16.070
using our Google
Container Engine,

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:17.610
which is based on Kubernetes.

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:19.130
And there are a lot of
talks about Kubernetes.

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:20.463
And you guys should definitely--

00:25:20.463 --> 00:25:22.580
I would recommend
going, and check it out.

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:26.690
But Kubernetes is a container
orchestration system.

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:28.760
And we have a hosted
version of Kubernetes

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:31.910
which is called Google
Container Engine.

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:33.680
And what we're going
to show you guys here

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:37.070
is we're going to get
this migration happening,

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:38.820
going from App Engine
to Container Engine.

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:40.400
So we're going to show that.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:43.850
And then we're going to now work
with the live debugging issue

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.890
that we talked
about, and see what

00:25:45.890 --> 00:25:49.550
happens if this application is
actually having the bug that we

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.680
can't replicate locally.

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:54.350
And logging as well,
which is a big problem.

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:59.270
So Patrick, let's drop down
one level on the infrastructure

00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:03.230
layer, and get some
debugging, and logging going.

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:04.960
Back to the demo, please.

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:07.060
Thank you.

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:07.990
PATRICK FLYNN: OK.

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:09.770
This was a fun little project.

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:14.150
Basically, figure out how do
we replicate the experience we

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:17.920
just showed you on App
Engine on Kubernetes

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:19.930
with the same
service integration

00:26:19.930 --> 00:26:21.550
that app engine offers.

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:23.920
For instance, the Cloud
Debugger, and Google Cloud

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:26.320
Logging, and Google Trace.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:29.560
All those products should
just magically work.

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:32.230
With GKE you have your
own configuration.

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:34.910
You're sort of responsible
for making everything work.

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:39.040
That turned out most of
this is really easy to do.

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.920
And the reason that I--

00:26:41.920 --> 00:26:44.470
basically I selected the
open JDK8 from Docker Hub

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:47.100
as my base image, which is
not the image I would normally

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:47.600
recommend.

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:51.850
I would recommend using our own
Google curated images, which

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:54.280
do a lot of extra work to make
sure the configurations are

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:57.250
right, and they're updated
for security patches

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:58.900
on a weekly basis.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:01.570
But I just want to show that
with a plain vanilla image--

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:03.040
AMIR ROUZROKH:
Right, and I think

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:04.330
that's a good example of--

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.163
PATRICK FLYNN: and
minimal configuration you

00:27:06.163 --> 00:27:08.410
can actually deploy the
same app on to Kubernetes,

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:11.300
and have integration to
the rest of our services.

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:12.060
AMIR ROUZROKH: Patrick,
I was going to say,

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:13.562
like, this is a
good example of you

00:27:13.562 --> 00:27:16.165
know, you can just, go to a
different runtime, if you want.

00:27:16.165 --> 00:27:17.540
There's nothing
holding you back.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:18.081
So all right.

00:27:18.081 --> 00:27:19.510
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:24.400
So if you see here, all we're
doing is using the base image.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:28.840
Exposing 8080, downloading
the Cloud Debugger Agent,

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:31.300
which is open source
on GitHub, by the way,

00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:35.350
and configuring it in the agent
path of the Java invocation.

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:40.630
So this is what a typical Spring
boot Docker image would have.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:44.340
Java and then the app.

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:47.585
But there's an extra step
that we have to do, because--

00:27:47.585 --> 00:27:49.960
we're going to be talking
about the Cloud Debugger later,

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:55.020
but one thing that App Engine
offers that Raw Infrastructure

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.900
Environment does not is
the service inversion model

00:27:57.900 --> 00:28:01.230
so that you can identify
a large set of instances

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:03.930
as providing the
same job, right?

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:05.190
Providing the same binary.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:07.200
Doing the same thing.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:10.890
We need to do that for our
diagnostics integration.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:15.540
So what I've done here
is used Maven filtering

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:17.790
to actually filter
the service name

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:20.130
and project version
properties from the project

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:23.110
into the Docker file, OK?

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:25.915
And let me just quickly show
you the POM XML as well.

00:28:28.495 --> 00:28:30.870
You guys are going get tired
of this, but how many of you

00:28:30.870 --> 00:28:32.765
are using Maven regularly?

00:28:32.765 --> 00:28:33.950
Yeah.

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:36.080
Gradle, out of curiosity?

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:36.600
OK.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:38.422
It's more or less
what I expected.

00:28:38.422 --> 00:28:39.880
AMIR ROUZROKH: So
it's like 70, 30?

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:40.590
PATRICK FLYNN: It's like 70, 30.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:41.740
Something like that.

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.670
So we have Gradle
and Maven plug-ins.

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:46.760
So everybody is covered.

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:52.180
In Maven there are various
ways that you can build

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:54.690
Docker images from a Java app.

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:57.919
So we showed you earlier
that with App Engine Flex

00:28:57.919 --> 00:28:59.960
you don't actually need
to worry about that part.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:02.230
But if you're deploying
to Kubernetes.

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:04.735
We're no longer taking care
of the Docker build for you.

00:29:04.735 --> 00:29:06.730
You need to create the image.

00:29:06.730 --> 00:29:10.720
The way that I've done that is
I just took the Spotify Docker

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:11.650
Maven plug-in.

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:13.420
They have two of them.

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:14.880
They both work differently.

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:18.380
I just picked this one for
the purposes of this demo.

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:21.640
And the image name is
actually really important

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:26.980
because the Google Cloud
Platform offers a Docker image

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:29.474
Container Registry, called
the Google Container Registry.

00:29:29.474 --> 00:29:30.890
AMIR ROUZROKH:
That's a good name.

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:34.010
PATRICK FLYNN: G star dot io.

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:39.160
Name your image after G star dot
io slash your project and then

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:42.610
your artifact name and any
tags you want to add to it.

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:44.275
So we do that here.

00:29:44.275 --> 00:29:46.900
And then once you've built your
Docker image all you need to do

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:49.570
is push it to Container
Registry, which

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:51.790
is as easy as calling--

00:29:51.790 --> 00:29:53.080
you should see here--

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:57.350
G Cloud Docker push
the name of your image.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:07.670
I've got two layers of
zooming going on right now.

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:09.160
All right.

00:30:09.160 --> 00:30:11.730
So I can show you--

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.360
what I've actually done
here is kind of neat.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:19.030
I've bound exec-ing
the G Cloud Docker

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:22.720
push to the deploy phase
of my Maven project,

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:26.380
because if you think about
it, the Maven life cycles,

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:28.390
deploy is about
publishing your artifacts.

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:33.130
And your artifact, when your
Docker base is no longer a Java

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:36.880
artifact it's Doctor image.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:38.890
So if I kick that
off what'll happen

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:43.960
is we will build the application
filter, the docker file.

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:47.570
Create a staging directory,
and Docker build that.

00:30:47.570 --> 00:30:50.429
And then push that to
Google Container Registry.

00:30:50.429 --> 00:30:52.220
AMIR ROUZROKH: So
Patrick, if I could have,

00:30:52.220 --> 00:30:55.790
if I have another container
that I've built previously

00:30:55.790 --> 00:30:58.370
on another platform,
or another place,

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:01.430
I could just point to that,
and deploy that on Kubernetes,

00:31:01.430 --> 00:31:02.210
and GKE, right?

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:03.710
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah,
with containers

00:31:03.710 --> 00:31:05.330
there's a whole lot of
stuff that you could do.

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:06.320
AMIR ROUZROKH: Right.

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:08.611
PATRICK FLYNN: One thing that
we didn't mention earlier

00:31:08.611 --> 00:31:12.170
is that App Engine Flex also
uses Google Container Registry.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:13.970
So I'm going to
take you over there.

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:16.250
And you can take a look.

00:31:16.250 --> 00:31:20.480
This is the Google Cloud Console
and the Container Registry

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:21.170
page.

00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:24.230
You can actually see all
of our App Engine builds

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:25.710
show up in here.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.740
So we can see the
previous deployments

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:30.280
that we did right there.

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:35.080
These images are
deployable to Kubernetes.

00:31:40.301 --> 00:31:41.800
Now, if you take a
look at demo app,

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:46.240
which is the image that we
just pushed, we have v5.8,

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:51.060
which is actually the
image from 22 minutes ago.

00:31:51.060 --> 00:31:52.650
I pushed an earlier example.

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.090
So this is the one
that you were seeing.

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:58.350
I didn't make any modifications.

00:31:58.350 --> 00:31:58.950
All right.

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:03.570
So this service is actually
running on Kubernetes news

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:05.100
at the moment.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:07.870
If we go to the Container Engine
page and look at our cluster--

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:09.660
I created this cluster earlier.

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:11.850
Similar to App Engine
Flex, it's built on VM.

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:14.220
So it takes some time to start.

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:14.790
Not too long.

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:17.570
Just a few minutes.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:22.070
We then created a
Kubernetes deployment

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:25.500
on that cluster
that has three pods.

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:27.800
And I initially
pre-configured it

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:30.710
to be serving the 5.7
version of our app.

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:33.590
I can go over here,
just reload it,

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:35.540
and you can see
that there's v5.7.

00:32:35.540 --> 00:32:38.430
So what we'll do is
deploy version 5.8,

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:39.875
which we just pushed to GCR.

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:47.350
That's a relatively easy
thing to do in Kubernetes.

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:49.565
You can just go
and edit your spec.

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:59.650
So let me just zoom
in here a little bit.

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:04.440
You see the image that's being
used by the pod is demo app

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:05.270
v5.7.

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:07.860
All I need to do is
just change it to 5.8,

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:11.342
save it, and then close.

00:33:11.342 --> 00:33:12.590
OK.

00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:16.110
And now my deployment
has been edited.

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:18.820
So at that point the
Kubernetes master

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:21.400
knows that the
deployment that it's got

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:23.160
doesn't match the spec anymore.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:25.540
It's going to bring
down the pods,

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:28.360
bring up new pods
with the new version.

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:31.810
And in a few
moments, just as long

00:33:31.810 --> 00:33:33.820
as it takes for a
Spring Boot to get

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:37.750
started you'll be able to
start surfing traffic again.

00:33:37.750 --> 00:33:40.780
You can configure Kubernetes to
do rolling upgrades, as well,

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:43.960
so that you have no downtime
between swapping versions

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:44.710
that you see here.

00:33:44.710 --> 00:33:46.460
That v5.8 is now running.

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:48.126
AMIR ROUZROKH: So
Patrick, you basically

00:33:48.126 --> 00:33:51.070
did what we did in App
Engine, going from 5.7 to 5.8

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:52.820
just with all of our control.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:53.850
So you know--

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:55.600
PATRICK FLYNN: It's a
more manual process.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:56.160
AMIR ROUZROKH: Yep.

00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:56.950
It's a more managed process.

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:57.825
PATRICK FLYNN: Right.

00:34:00.634 --> 00:34:02.610
OK.

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:05.150
So the next thing that we're
going to be showing you

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:08.659
is, as I showed you
earlier, in the Docker image

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:12.239
we configured the
Cloud Debugger agent.

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:16.170
The debugger agent
is a necessary piece

00:34:16.170 --> 00:34:20.190
of the Stackdriver debugger,
or the Google Cloud Debugger,

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:23.535
which is a really exciting
product that Google provides.

00:34:26.769 --> 00:34:27.269
Amir.

00:34:27.269 --> 00:34:28.110
AMIR ROUZROKH: Yes.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:30.109
PATRICK FLYNN: Earlier--
sorry about that, Amir.

00:34:30.109 --> 00:34:34.620
Amir, Earlier on asked
you guys how many of you

00:34:34.620 --> 00:34:38.087
had actually had a hard
to debug production issue.

00:34:38.087 --> 00:34:39.420
It's happened to me a few times.

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:42.210
And it happens especially
at scale, right?

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:46.380
When you have a large
cluster under a lot of load

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:48.989
a lot of bugs can
pop up, corner cases,

00:34:48.989 --> 00:34:52.860
that you simply don't have the
logging in place to effectively

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:53.909
diagnose.

00:34:53.909 --> 00:34:56.429
And you don't have
a lot of options

00:34:56.429 --> 00:35:00.210
for debugging, because you can't
attach a debugger in production

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:01.590
for a couple of reasons.

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:04.680
You don't want to
actually block requests

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:06.630
coming into an instance.

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:09.540
And if you have
100,000 nodes you

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:12.100
don't know where that
bug is going to happen.

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:13.950
You can't connect
to all of them.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:16.530
So the Cloud Debugger
is a piece of technology

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:20.820
that Google developed internally
for our purposes, which

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:23.430
solves this problem.

00:35:23.430 --> 00:35:25.980
The way that it works is that
when you set a breakpoint

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:31.450
in the Cloud Debugger there's
a Cloud Debugger master that

00:35:31.450 --> 00:35:34.637
monitors all the agents
running for a specific service

00:35:34.637 --> 00:35:36.220
and notifies them
that they need to be

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:39.070
looking for this
particular break point.

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:42.760
The second any traffic
on any of the instances

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:45.850
actually hit that line
they'll notify the master.

00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:48.890
And eventually you'll
be notified, as well.

00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:51.550
They won't break execution.

00:35:51.550 --> 00:35:55.150
They'll just capture
state in the stack frame

00:35:55.150 --> 00:35:57.460
and store it in a database.

00:35:57.460 --> 00:35:58.960
AMIR ROUZROKH: So
basically, I don't

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:03.820
have to worry about having
down times, and maintenance,

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:05.871
windows, and all that
sort of stuff anymore.

00:36:05.871 --> 00:36:06.370
OK.

00:36:06.370 --> 00:36:07.850
PATRICK FLYNN: That's right.

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:13.850
So let's actually go and
show what kind of bug

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:15.897
we want to diagnose.

00:36:15.897 --> 00:36:16.480
Let's go back.

00:36:20.202 --> 00:36:23.330
Here you go.

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:24.250
Something's not right.

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:27.830
And I don't know if you guys
can figure out what it is.

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:33.110
But the site is telling me that
my browser is Firefox, which

00:36:33.110 --> 00:36:35.450
is a great browser, by the way.

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:39.290
But the browser that I'm
using is Google Chrome.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:41.002
AMIR ROUZROKH: Looks
like Chrome to me.

00:36:41.002 --> 00:36:41.501
Yeah.

00:36:41.501 --> 00:36:42.667
PATRICK FLYNN: That's a bug.

00:36:42.667 --> 00:36:44.230
AMIR ROUZROKH: That is a bug.

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:46.730
PATRICK FLYNN: So let's use the
Cloud Debugger to figure out

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:48.230
what's going on.

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:52.340
This is happening on
our Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:52.340 --> 00:36:55.127
You can start the Cloud Debugger
just by finding it over here.

00:36:55.127 --> 00:36:56.960
AMIR ROUZROKH: Before
you go there, Patrick,

00:36:56.960 --> 00:36:59.270
can you show the code that's
actually making this happen?

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:00.353
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah, sure.

00:37:00.353 --> 00:37:02.280
AMIR ROUZROKH: Maybe we
could just eyeball it.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:02.780
All right.

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:04.100
The fuser agent is Mozilla Fox.

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:05.600
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah,
we're basically

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:08.310
serving the hello path.

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:10.810
We're getting the user agent.

00:37:10.810 --> 00:37:14.050
Checking it's contents
for a specific string.

00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:16.944
If we see it, we figure out
what browser type it is.

00:37:16.944 --> 00:37:18.610
And it looks like it
makes sense, right?

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:19.870
Mozilla is Firefox.

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:20.810
Explorer's Explorer.

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:23.313
Chrome is Chrome.

00:37:23.313 --> 00:37:26.140
Cool.

00:37:26.140 --> 00:37:28.902
So I have a Google Cloud
Debug run configuration.

00:37:28.902 --> 00:37:30.360
Since you're IntelliJ
users, you're

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:34.030
all familiar with the way that
debugging works in IntelliJ.

00:37:34.030 --> 00:37:35.636
You have the run configuration.

00:37:35.636 --> 00:37:36.760
You click the debug button.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:44.800
This is the Kubernetes
version that I had.

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:48.030
I'm going to attach to it.

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:51.410
I have a old watch
point from earlier on.

00:37:51.410 --> 00:37:52.250
Going to delete it.

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:58.476
Hold on a second.

00:37:58.476 --> 00:37:59.350
Let's try that again.

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:03.530
OK.

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:04.620
So you're going to--

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:07.490
I was practicing this earlier
and I had some previous states.

00:38:07.490 --> 00:38:09.115
AMIR ROUZROKH: No,
I was going, sort of

00:38:09.115 --> 00:38:12.300
just kind of listen in line
52 where Firefox is displayed

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.925
to see what's happening.

00:38:13.925 --> 00:38:15.675
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah,
so we know that we're

00:38:15.675 --> 00:38:18.480
seeing Firefox, which means that
line of code is being executed.

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:20.460
Let's find out what's going on.

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:22.880
We're going to set the snapshot.

00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:25.200
When the check mark
appears that means

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:29.090
that the master has confirmation
that that's a valid line.

00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:31.557
We're going to go
and trigger the bug.

00:38:31.557 --> 00:38:33.890
We don't know where the load
balancer sent that request.

00:38:33.890 --> 00:38:35.930
It went to any of
the three nodes

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:38.950
that we've got configured.

00:38:38.950 --> 00:38:44.160
And if we go over here
we can see the state.

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:50.570
So if you look at
user-agent, guess what?

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:55.740
The user-agent of Chrome
reports itself as Mozilla.

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:58.080
This is something
that browsers do

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:00.600
because of legacy, all right?

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:03.270
But with the Cloud Debugger it
was really easy to figure out

00:39:03.270 --> 00:39:04.829
what was going on.

00:39:04.829 --> 00:39:07.120
AMIR ROUZROKH: So you basically
got a whole stack trace

00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:10.100
whenever that part of
the code was touched,

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:11.876
and that's really good for--

00:39:11.876 --> 00:39:13.750
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah, I
want to just point out

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:17.920
that the debugger is very safe.

00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:19.960
It doesn't slow
down the execution,

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:22.510
or it's a really trivial amount.

00:39:22.510 --> 00:39:25.720
We use it at Google
in production.

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:27.280
It only captures
the first snapshot

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:29.200
and then it disables itself.

00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:33.590
So you'll get one sample of
what you were looking for.

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:36.530
In the future we're probably
going to provide you

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:37.380
with more options.

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:38.463
But for now it's just one.

00:39:38.463 --> 00:39:41.860
AMIR ROUZROKH: But Patrick,
like, this is really cool ,

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:45.170
but you know, sometimes I don't
know when the trigger happens,

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:47.080
because it could
be an edge case.

00:39:47.080 --> 00:39:48.957
Do I just, you know, wait there?

00:39:48.957 --> 00:39:50.165
Can you show us a better way?

00:39:50.165 --> 00:39:51.110
PATRICK FLYNN: I can.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:51.800
I can.

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:55.720
So let's stop listening.

00:39:55.720 --> 00:39:59.070
And imagine a real
scenario where

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:01.800
this is happening, where this
sort of hard to reproduce bug

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:03.390
suddenly gets reported.

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:07.230
Most of the time you're not just
waiting in the version of code

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.720
that's executing in production.

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:12.300
You're doing some
feature development.

00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:19.610
You've moved over to
version 9 and you've

00:40:19.610 --> 00:40:24.870
added like some
random out, or hellos.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:27.890
I'm really creative here.

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:30.580
And then somebody comes and
tells you, hey, guess what?

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:32.330
We're having this crazy
bug in production.

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:33.660
We have no idea what's going on.

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:35.390
AMIR ROUZROKH: And that's
for version 5.8, by the way,

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:36.580
so you've moved on from that.

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:37.455
PATRICK FLYNN: Right.

00:40:37.455 --> 00:40:40.940
So we actually
capture source context

00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:43.370
at build time and deploy
it with the application,

00:40:43.370 --> 00:40:46.040
both in App Engine, and
we configured things such

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:49.760
that we do it for
Kubernetes as well.

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:53.370
Which means that when you
connect we've actually

00:40:53.370 --> 00:40:55.140
identified that the
version of your code--

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:57.380
let me zoom in here--

00:40:57.380 --> 00:41:01.720
is not the same as the version
that's running in production.

00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:03.850
And whether you've
committed things or not,

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:07.720
or you have unstaged changes,
we'll take all your changes,

00:41:07.720 --> 00:41:11.010
stash them, and put
you on the version

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:13.304
of the code that makes
sense for the service

00:41:13.304 --> 00:41:14.220
that you're debugging.

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:17.140
So let's do that.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:19.090
We're going to get
rid of this guy,

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:24.340
because like I said earlier,
it got caught already.

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:25.830
And we're going to create--

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:26.350
oops.

00:41:26.350 --> 00:41:28.105
I didn't mean to go there.

00:41:28.105 --> 00:41:29.230
Create the new break point.

00:41:29.230 --> 00:41:31.510
But at this point
this bug is always

00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:33.010
going to happen
with Chrome browser.

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:36.564
But for a bug that happens
once every blue moon

00:41:36.564 --> 00:41:38.230
due to some race
condition, or whatever,

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:40.480
you're certainly not going
to stop here and leave

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:44.000
your IDE in this state,
waiting for it to happen.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.030
So what you can do
is you can stop.

00:41:46.030 --> 00:41:49.750
And instead of telling the
debugger to stop listening,

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:51.395
you can tell it to continue.

00:41:51.395 --> 00:41:53.380
At that point we know
that you want to go back

00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:54.550
to your old workflow.

00:41:54.550 --> 00:41:58.285
We offer to check out the
branch that you were on,

00:41:58.285 --> 00:41:59.590
and un-stash your changes.

00:41:59.590 --> 00:42:01.170
So we'll go ahead and do that.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:06.940
And if we go here we see that
we're back to version 5.9.

00:42:06.940 --> 00:42:09.160
So now if this gets triggered--

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:11.930
going to come back here--

00:42:11.930 --> 00:42:16.490
we notify you in the IDE
that your hard to reproduce

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:19.910
bug has happened and you can
continue your debug session.

00:42:22.459 --> 00:42:24.250
And if you look at the
version of the code,

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:28.490
now it's back to v5.8.

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:31.940
Many times I have been
tearing my hair out

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:34.670
because I've attached my
debugger to a version of code

00:42:34.670 --> 00:42:37.470
that is different from the one
that my editor is showing me.

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:39.440
It happens all the time.

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:44.440
And it can take forever to
figure out what's going on,

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:46.974
especially if you have lines
that kind of match, right?

00:42:46.974 --> 00:42:49.390
In terms of, they are executing,
but they're not the lines

00:42:49.390 --> 00:42:51.560
that you thought.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:52.060
OK.

00:42:52.060 --> 00:42:52.900
That's cool.

00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:56.140
AMIR ROUZROKH: So Patrick,
this is all great.

00:42:56.140 --> 00:42:58.780
You know, another one of the
scenarios that I can think of

00:42:58.780 --> 00:43:04.220
is I have deployed my version
5.8 as you've shown over here,

00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:10.090
but I forgot that I would
like to log a certain value,

00:43:10.090 --> 00:43:14.500
or a certain field over time.

00:43:14.500 --> 00:43:16.420
I've moved on many,
many versions.

00:43:16.420 --> 00:43:19.150
And you know, it's going to
be quite the hassle for me

00:43:19.150 --> 00:43:22.750
to go back and deploy a new
version due to changed code.

00:43:22.750 --> 00:43:25.299
Maybe you can show us the way
that we can do this better?

00:43:25.299 --> 00:43:26.590
PATRICK FLYNN: I'd be happy to.

00:43:26.590 --> 00:43:29.500
This is a scenario
I'd like to call,

00:43:29.500 --> 00:43:35.560
oops I forgot to write logging
statements in my application.

00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:38.080
This is a scenario
that helps with bugs.

00:43:38.080 --> 00:43:40.600
It also is a scenario
that helps with people

00:43:40.600 --> 00:43:42.790
who might be using logging
to generate metrics

00:43:42.790 --> 00:43:44.380
about their application.

00:43:44.380 --> 00:43:47.404
Maybe piping their cloud
logs into big data, right?

00:43:47.404 --> 00:43:48.820
Into like, BigQuery,
for instance.

00:43:48.820 --> 00:43:50.230
AMIR ROUZROKH: Right.

00:43:50.230 --> 00:43:53.360
PATRICK FLYNN: So
in this scenario

00:43:53.360 --> 00:43:56.330
we're actually using the
same cloud debugger agent

00:43:56.330 --> 00:44:00.380
to insert dynamically log
statements into your production

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:02.920
app without requiring
you to redeploy.

00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:05.324
And now every time
that line gets executed

00:44:05.324 --> 00:44:06.740
on anywhere in
your cluster you're

00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:08.520
going to see it in your logs.

00:44:08.520 --> 00:44:12.330
So we don't have integration
into the IDES for this feature,

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:12.830
yet.

00:44:12.830 --> 00:44:16.100
But we're working on it.

00:44:16.100 --> 00:44:18.590
You can use the Cloud
SDK G Cloud commands

00:44:18.590 --> 00:44:19.754
to create these log points.

00:44:19.754 --> 00:44:21.670
If you Take a look over
here-- let me zoom in.

00:44:21.670 --> 00:44:25.010
AMIR ROUZROKH: Patrick, can
show the line item in the code

00:44:25.010 --> 00:44:26.100
that you're trying to log?

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:26.933
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah.

00:44:26.933 --> 00:44:28.670
That's an excellent idea.

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:29.720
OK.

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:30.470
So let's see.

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:32.960
Line 37.

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:38.790
I forgot to mention
that we have some really

00:44:38.790 --> 00:44:42.450
nice hand-crafted Java libraries
that are coming out in beta,

00:44:42.450 --> 00:44:46.980
at the moment, for some of our
main Google Cloud Services,

00:44:46.980 --> 00:44:48.150
like Datastore.

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:49.290
And I'm using that here.

00:44:49.290 --> 00:44:52.090
You just important
in your POM XML,

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:53.980
and you can write to Datastore.

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:57.040
Datastore is a really
good idea for people

00:44:57.040 --> 00:45:02.370
who expect to have
really large data, right?

00:45:02.370 --> 00:45:06.440
App Engine Flex can help
you scale your computation.

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:09.430
But it can't help you
scale your database.

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:11.710
You need to use a scalable
database if you really

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:14.590
expect large scale,
and Datastore

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:17.230
is a database that scales
horizontally automatically

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:17.730
for you.

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:22.340
It's sort of like the
App Engine for databases.

00:45:22.340 --> 00:45:24.760
So we have this system
output line here.

00:45:24.760 --> 00:45:28.690
But Really what I wanted
to do is figure out

00:45:28.690 --> 00:45:34.360
every time I saved a data
store I want to be notified

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:35.980
that save has occurred.

00:45:35.980 --> 00:45:38.650
So at line 37 you see
that we're actually

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:40.111
just returning the greeting.

00:45:40.111 --> 00:45:41.110
Let me hit the endpoint.

00:45:41.110 --> 00:45:41.985
I can show it to you.

00:45:47.810 --> 00:45:48.400
All right.

00:45:48.400 --> 00:45:50.080
We're just generating
a time stamp,

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:53.710
saving it to the database,
reading it, showing it to you.

00:45:53.710 --> 00:45:55.780
This is demo purposes only.

00:45:55.780 --> 00:45:58.251
Not particularly useful.

00:45:58.251 --> 00:46:01.600
Now, if we go to Cloud Logging--

